  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nUL high is centered well to our east, toward TN/KY, but mid- \nlevel dry air and not so great thermodynamics (MLCAPE of 250-500 \nJ/kg) have greatly limited convection, keeping it to the Sacs and \nHudspeth County. This pattern looks to change little for tomorrow,\nso thunderstorms should largely be limited to the same areas. By \nThursday, moisture will begin to increase from southwest to \nnortheast, which will bring an increase in shower and thunderstorm\nchances and coverage. The GFS and Euro are in disagreement with \nhow quickly we will moisten up with the GFS much quicker and \ntherefore showing more QPF on Thursday. Either way, it appears \nmoisture will peak on Friday. NBM mean shows values peaking \nbetween 1.35-1.45\", and thus, heavy rain will be the main concern \nwith storms. \n\nThe moisture sticks around for Saturday, but the 500 mb high will\nbegin reforming over Southern NM, warming temperatures throughout\nthe column. This pattern will reduce overall instability and \ntherefore limit thunderstorm coverage. I suspect given PW values \naround 1.3\" we'll still see scattered mountain and isolated \nstorms. \n\nThe UL high remains nearly stationary to start next week while PW\nvalues drift downward thanks to mid-level drying. NBM mean PW \ndrops from around 1.35\" to around 1.25\" by next Tuesday. The best \nmoisture will remain west of the Continental Divide and that will \nbe where we see our best rain and storm chances. Regardless, we \nwill return to a hit and miss style forecast with most of us \nmissing. \n\nTemperatures look to largely hold steady throughout the period \nwith the NBM keeping highs in the mid to upper 90s in the \nlowlands, which is about 5 degrees above normal. Ultimately though\nit will depend on how much drying occurs and the strength of the \nhigh next week if we see El Paso add to its tally of 100 degree \ndays. Conversely, with the increased moisture Friday we may see \nhighs come in a bit closer to normal.",
